# Staged multiple-data-blob restic packs

This isolated stage changes native regular-file snapshot capture to batch new data blobs into genuine encrypted restic v1/v2 packs. Data blobs are independently encrypted/authenticated; the encrypted pack header describes every stored length and plaintext SHA-256 ID. Index rows use matching offsets, lengths, types and optional v2 uncompressed lengths. Trees remain separate, one tree per pack. The original executable is only an independent test oracle, never a runtime wrapper.

The default target includes payloads, header and trailer within 16 MiB. A pack also stops at 4,096 records. The sequential accumulator owns encrypted buffers, verifies/reuses pending duplicates, and discards pending buffers on failed capture. Prior indexed blobs are authenticated before reuse. All completed data/tree packs precede index publication; that index precedes its referencing snapshot. A failed capture can retain immutable unreferenced completed packs/tree packs; repair/prune is still absent.

Existing bounds remain: 8 MiB Rabin data chunks, 64 MiB per file, 512 MiB per snapshot, 96 MiB read pack bound, 16 MiB per metadata/tree document, 64 MiB cumulative stored/decoded metadata/tree bytes, 10,000 entries, 100,000 index records, 4,096 packs, 100 directory levels, 1,024 content IDs per file, 16 keys, 4 GiB aggregate pack reads and a two-minute cooperative native-operation deadline. The new writer checks accepted Buffer types/lengths before copying. Native HTTP operations still do not support cancellation; the file adapter and writer guard tests exercise private cooperative cancellation separately. Deadline guards do not preempt synchronous crypto/compression or blocked I/O. Cleanup still validates confinement without allowing elapsed deadlines to reverse committed snapshot success; primary failures and secondary cleanup warnings remain observable.

Memory is not limited to output size: a data queue retains at most about 16 MiB of ciphertext, with at most 4,096 row records. Flush allocates a second packed buffer up to 16 MiB plus small header buffers; the next encoded/encrypted chunk can coexist while an older queue flushes. Source assembly/output chunks, compression/decompression buffers, AES temporary copies, metadata/index objects, the existing most-recent 96 MiB read-pack cache and up to 144 MiB scrypt allocation also contribute. GC/Node/libzstd overhead is not measured. These bounds are app guards, not process/OS memory isolation.

Implementation is source-assisted from restic 0.19.1 commit `6aa3a516ce654808a1f28f9fa21e9b7c8e6e90bf`; exact BSD-2-Clause notice and pinned files/URLs/SHA identities are in `source-provenance.json` and `references/*.snapshot`. Format binding: `doc/design.rst` lines 152–222 (pack layout), 248–308 (index rows), 647–682 (publication/read ordering); `internal/repository/pack/pack.go` Add/Finalize/makeHeader; index/index.go packJSON/blobJSON/generatePackList; repository.go pack defaults/separate data/tree managers; packer_manager.go SaveBlob/Flush/pickPacker. Those algorithms inform the contract; no original Go helper was executed as the new runtime. Restic's concurrent pack selection, temporary pack streaming/merging, randomization, exact encoder output and broader product behavior are not reproduced.

Run `node verify-stage.mjs` from this stage or by its absolute path. The focused collector authenticates the selected frozen production baseline, retains exact run-source snapshots and raw stdout/stderr, and publishes only inside this ignored stage. Oracle binaries are local test inputs under `projects/09-restic/artifacts` and must be excluded from public evidence. The original ZIP SHA-256 is `da948ad707ed690426473aaba2046cd61f8f90f6f0e7dab6be0d5796531de67d`; executable SHA-256 is `b0dd1fd21eea5d8fe1325f55f7118213c21f36de8a261e04c0624a5ab9fd7830`.

Finite checks cover v1/off and every v2 compression mode, duplicate files and repeated 8 MiB chunks, prior-snapshot reuse, authentic original multi-blob input and native append, original check/read/restore and digest-matched native restores, exact header/index rows, target/record rollover, borrowed-buffer mutation, failure poisoning, pre-commit deadline/cleanup and malformed authenticated second-blob offsets. Existing compression/Rabin/owned lifecycle regressions are retained; the old corruption helper is adapted to keep later payloads and update their offsets. Setup failures and exact earlier versions remain under `failures/` and `runs/`. There is no timing, efficiency-performance, cryptographic-security, full-product parity or superiority claim.
